<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/>
<title>From the first races to 1899 · Cycling’s pioneer years</title><meta name="description" content="From the safety bicycle and pneumatic tyre to Paris–Brest–Paris, solo six-day races and Major Taylor: cycling’s pioneer years to 1899."/><link rel="canonical" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href={canonical}/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href="https://teob.dk/perioder/1800-1899/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://teob.dk/perioder/1800-1899/"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB"/><meta property="og:site_name" content="The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:title" content="From the first races to 1899 · Cycling’s pioneer years"/><meta property="og:description" content="The bicycle found its modern outline as newspapers, velodromes and long road races created a new sport."/><meta property="og:url" content={canonical}/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1900-1920/wild-west/track-racing.webp"/><meta property="og:image:alt" content="Early track racers on a velodrome"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1800-1899/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page">
  <header class="wildwest-hero"><div><p class="section-label">From the first races to 1899</p><h1>Cycling’s wild <em>pioneer years</em></h1></div><p>The safety bicycle was new, roads were poor and rules were often written after the race. Yet cycling rapidly became transport, industry and spectator sport.</p></header>
  <figure class="decade1920-hero-image"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Illustrated crowd and riders at the start of Paris–Brest–Paris in 1891" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>The Paris–Brest–Paris start outside <em>Le Petit Journal</em> in 1891. Contemporary illustration from the newspaper’s supplement, public domain via Wikimedia Commons.</figcaption></figure>
  <PeriodStructure language="en" period="1800-1899"/>
  <section class="wildwest-lead"><p class="section-label">Before the grand tours</p><p>Cycle racing did not begin with the Tour de France. Before 1900 there were already long newspaper races, crowded velodromes, professional specialists and a bicycle industry learning through invention and failure.</p></section>
  <section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">From city to city</p><h2>The road was part of the resistance</h2></div><div><p>Early road races ran from one city to another. Paris–Rouen in 1869 is often counted among the first major events, followed in the 1890s by Paris–Brest–Paris, Liège–Bastogne–Liège and Paris–Roubaix.</p><p>Courses crossed gravel, wheel ruts and loose stones. Rain turned roads into mud, and a puncture cost far more than a modern wheel change. The rider had to stay awake, find the route and make the bicycle usable again.</p><p>Newspapers were not neutral observers. They created races to create news and sell copies. Rider, bicycle and media became parts of the same new spectacle.</p></div></section>
  <section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">The velodrome</p><h2>Six days with very little sleep</h2></div><div><p>The first six-day races were solo contests. One rider covered as many laps as possible over six days, often riding 18–20 hours each day. Spectators came and went while the rider continued under electric light.</p><p>Sleep deprivation became part of the show. Accounts describe riders hallucinating, weaving or being helped from the track. Teddy Hale won the major Madison Square Garden race in 1896 after more than 3,000 kilometres. The feat was admired, but the format was also condemned as brutal entertainment.</p><p>The six-day race later became a two-rider team event. It did not make the sport gentle, but the riders could at least take turns being awake.</p></div></section>
  <section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">The machine takes shape</p><h2>Two equal wheels and air in the tyres</h2></div><div><p>During the 1880s, the safety bicycle established chain drive to the rear wheel and two similarly sized wheels. Its basic layout still resembles the modern bicycle. It was a substantial advance from the high wheeler and its long fall.</p><p>The pneumatic tyre made rough roads less destructive to speed and body. Brakes, freewheels, chains and gearing were still developing. Some solutions worked; others quickly disappeared.</p><p>Fixed gears were common, so the pedals continued to turn whenever the wheel turned. Practical gear changing still lay ahead. On a long race, a spanner could therefore matter almost as much as a strong sprint.</p></div></section>
  <figure class="decade1920-wide"><OptimizedImage src={terront} alt="Charles Terront with his safety bicycle after Paris–Brest–Paris in 1891" widths={[480,760,1100]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 80vw"/><figcaption>Charles Terront and his bicycle on the cover of <em>Le Petit Journal</em> after the 1891 victory. Illustration by Henri Meyer, public domain.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Food, medicine and belief</p><h2>The body was kept going with what people knew</h2></div><div><p>Sports nutrition did not exist as a discipline. Riders ate ordinary heavy food and drank coffee, cocoa, beer or wine. In very long races, the immediate task was to consume something and continue.</p><p>Stimulants and painkillers faced no prohibited list. Caffeine, alcohol and stronger substances occupied an unclear territory between medicine, alertness and belief. Later accounts mention cocaine, strychnine and ether, although practices varied and many details are better documented in the first decades after 1900.</p><p>What is clear is that the boundary between care and performance enhancement was not the boundary the sport later tried to draw.</p></div></section>
  <section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">1899</p><h2>Major Taylor won despite the sport around him</h2></div><div><p>Marshall “Major” Taylor became world sprint champion in Montreal in 1899. He was 20 years old and became one of the first Black world champions in international sport.</p><p>The result tells only part of the story. Taylor was excluded from tracks, threatened, assaulted and deliberately blocked in races. He did not win in a sport that had made room for him. He won while parts of that sport actively tried to keep him out.</p><p>By the end of the century, cycling was already international, commercial and technically curious. It was also marked by the inequalities of its time. Both characteristics followed it into the new century.</p></div></section>
  <section class="period-feature"><div><p class="section-label">Stories from the period</p><h2>Three days on the road</h2><p>Continue with the individual story of Charles Terront and the first Paris–Brest–Paris.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links">{relatedStories.map((story)=><a href={`${base}/en/stories/${story.slug}/`}><span>Related story · {story.year}</span><strong>{story.title}</strong><b>Read the story →</b></a>)}</div></section>
  <nav class="wildwest-next" aria-label="Further navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/periods/`}>← All periods</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1900-1920/`}>Next period: 1900–1920 →</a></nav>
</article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
